<ArticleLayout slug="maillard-reactions">
  <h2>What a Maillard reaction is</h2>
  <p>
    Proteins and sugars react together to form complexes that are
    indigestible in the rumen: bypass proteins. These Maillard compounds are
    dissolved thanks to the acid pH of the abomasum; the amino acids they
    contain therefore become fully available again for absorption in the
    intestine.
  </p>

  <h2>What triggers it</h2>
  <p>Maillard reactions occur:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>in the presence of amino acids and sugars,</li>
    <li>at temperatures between 0 and 150&nbsp;°C,</li>
    <li>at a pH between 6 and 10,</li>
    <li>at a moisture level of around 15%.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Temperature has a strong influence on the speed of the reaction: almost
    nothing happens up to 60&nbsp;°C, then everything speeds up.
  </p>

  <MaillardChart />

  <h2>Triggering or avoiding it, depending on the species</h2>
  <p>
    During extrusion cooking, it is therefore possible to adjust the
    parameters - temperature and residence time of the mix - in order to
    trigger Maillard reactions or not.
  </p>
  <h3>For ruminants: encourage them</h3>
  <p>
    It is worth encouraging these reactions for the production of ruminant
    feed: bypass proteins pass through the rumen without being degraded,
    then are digested in the intestine. On soybean, extrusion cooking
    increases the amount of bypass proteins by 100 to 300% - details in the
    article
    <a href="/en/expert-insights/benefits-of-extruded-soybean-for-livestock">
      The benefits of extruded soybean for livestock</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>For monogastrics: limit them</h3>
  <p>
    It will be important to limit them as far as possible for the production
    of poultry feed, for example: Maillard compounds reduce protein
    availability for monogastric species. The very short time of extrusion
    is what makes this possible - see
    <a href="/en/expert-insights/raw-material-processing-cereals">
      raw material processing: cereals</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Good control of extrusion cooking therefore makes it possible to vary the
    characteristics of the product according to the anatomy of the target
    species.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
